\begin_layout Title
Chapter 4: Production Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Proposition 48, Proposition 52, Problem 4, Problem 5,
 Problem 6, Problem 7, Problem 10.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this chapter we study production theory in a commodity space.
 First, we develop the mathematical representaiton of produciton units,
 which we refer simply as firms.
 Second, we characterize the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

behavior
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of firms whose objective is the maximizaiton of profits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Representation of firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We study an 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

-commodity world.
 Here, a firm is an economic entity that transforms commodities.
 We present a firm by means of the collection of bundles in the commodity
 space that are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

technically feasible
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
(13).
 
\series default
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $L=\left\{ \mbox{oranges, orange juice}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 We denote the generic amount of aranges, in pounds, by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and the generic amount of arange juice, in liters, by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 A given firm is able to produce from each pound of oranges, half a liter
 of juice, and cannot produce any oranges from orange juice.
 Moreover, the firm can dispose of any excess of orange juice.
 The mathematical representation of this firm is the set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y=\left\{ \left(-z_{1},y_{1}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}:z_{1}\geq0,y_{1}\leq\frac{1}{2}z_{1}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(97).

\series default
 
\emph on
A 
\series bold
production set
\emph default
 
\series default
\emph on
is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The generic production set is 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We refer to the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 as 
\series bold
feasible vectors
\series default
 (for 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

).
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

, commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is an input at 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{l}<0$
\end_inset

; symmetrically, it is an output whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{l}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following two examples will guid our study of production sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
(14).

\series default
 (Transformation Function).
 We define a production set by means of a function that tells us which vectors
 are feasible.
 No restriction is imposed on whether a commodity is an input or an output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F:\mathbb{R}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 be a function.
 The production set associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y^{F}\equiv\left\{ y\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:F(y)\leq0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

We refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 as a 
\series bold
transformation function
\series default
 and to the set 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\partial F\equiv\left\{ y\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:F(y)=0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

as the 
\series bold
transformation frontier for 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We define the 
\series bold
Marginal Rate of Transformation (MRT) of commodity l for commodity k at
 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\partial F$
\end_inset


\series default
 as the ratio:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRT_{lk}(y)\equiv\frac{\frac{\partial F}{\partial y_{l}}(y)}{\frac{\partial F}{\partial y_{k}}(y)}=-\frac{\Delta y_{k}}{\Delta y_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

With 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-axis and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

-axis, the MRT is a measure of how much the net output of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 can decrease if the firm increases the net output of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 by one marginal unit.
 The MRT of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 for commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is the absolute value of the slope of the transformation frontier on the
 
\begin_inset Formula $l-k$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

slide through 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the consumption space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The next example focuses on the single-output model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
(15).

\series default
 (Single-output model).
 We divide the commodity space into inputs and outputs.
 We define a production set by means of a funciton that tells us the amount
 of output that can be produced with a given input vector.
 For simplicity, we restrict to the single-output case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be a function.
 The production set associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y^{f}\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\equiv(z_{1},\ldots,z_{L-1})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z_{1},\ldots,z_{L-1})=0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

We refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 as a 
\series bold
production function
\series default
 and to the set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\partial f\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z_{1},\ldots,z_{L-1})=0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

as the 
\series bold
production frontier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We define the 
\series bold
Marginal Rate of Technical Substitution (MRTS) of input 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 for input 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset


\series default
 as the ratio:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRTS_{lk}(z)\equiv\frac{\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{l}}(z)}{\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{k}}(z)}=-\frac{\Delta z_{k}}{\Delta z_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

The single-output model admits an alternative representation of the production
 set by means of iso-quants.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-axis and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

-axis,, the MRTS measures the amount of input 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 that must be reduced to keep output level 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{q}=f(\bar{z})$
\end_inset

 when the amount of input 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 in increased marginally.
 The MRTS of input 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 for input 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is the absolute value of the slope of the iso-quant at 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Properties of production sets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be a production set.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is (or satisfies):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Non-empty 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Closed
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
No-free-lunch
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y:y\geq0\implies y=0$
\end_inset

.
 Alternatively, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\subseteq\{0\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Possibility of inaction
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Free-disposal
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ x\leq y:x\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Irreversible
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[y\in Y\ \wedge\ y\neq0\right]\implies-y\notin Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Non-increasing returns to scale (NI)
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Non-decreasing returns to scale (ND)
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in[1,+\infty):\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Constant returns to scale
\series default
 
\series bold
(CR)
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies both NI and ND returns to scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Additivity
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{y,y^{\prime}\}\subseteq Y:y+y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Convexity 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{-}^{2}\times\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 (single-output model).
 Let the production be defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(z_{1},z_{2})=z_{1}^{\alpha}z_{2}^{\beta}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha+\beta<1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies NI returns to scale.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha+\beta>1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies ND returns to scale.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha+\beta=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies CR to scale.
 Important to note, if 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is convex and 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies NI returns to scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(48).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{-}\times\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 (single-output model).
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies free-disposal and CR of scale, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $L=3$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{-}^{2}\times\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 (single-output model).
 Then, free-disposal and CR of scale may not imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is convex.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(49).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

is additive and satisfies NI returns to scale, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is a convex cone, i.e., for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y,y^{\prime}\}\subseteq Y$
\end_inset

 and each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha,\beta\}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y+\beta y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y,y^{\prime}\}\subseteq Y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha,\beta\}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, then NI returns to scale imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq1$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\alpha}{n}<1$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by NI returns to scale, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\alpha}{n}y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 By additivity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y=\frac{\alpha}{n}y+\cdots+\frac{\alpha}{n}y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 The symmetric argument shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 By additivy, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y+\beta y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(11).

\series default
 Additivity and NI returns to scale imply CR returns to scale and convexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This is a direct consequence of the proof and result of Proposition 49.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Profit maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this seciton we characterize the behavior of a price-taker profit maximizing
 firm.
 Consider a firm represented by production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The profit of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 when producing at a feasible vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot y\equiv\sum_{l\in L}p_{l}y_{l}
\]

\end_inset

A price-taker profit maximizing firm that observed prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 solves the 
\series bold
Profit Maximization Problem
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}}\ p\cdot y
\]

\end_inset

The solution of this problem and the maximum profit attained by the firm
 define the firm's supply correspondence and profit function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Supply correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(98).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be a production set.
 The 
\series bold
supply correspondence associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
y^{Y}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}^{L}\\
 & p & \mapsto & y^{Y}(p)\equiv\arg{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}}\ p\cdot y
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Profit function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(99).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be a production set.
 The 
\series bold
profit function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset


\series default
 is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\Pi^{Y}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & p & \mapsto & \Pi^{Y}(p)\equiv{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}}p\cdot y
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

Alternatively, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{Y}(p)\equiv p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in y^{Y}(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Profit maximizaiton: Transformation Function model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is described by means of a transformation function 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

, the firm's profit maximizaiton problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{\{y\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:F(y)\leq0\}}}\ p\cdot y
\]

\end_inset

We simplify notation and denote 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{F}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{F}$
\end_inset

 the supply correspondence and profit function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $Y^{F}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(50).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable transformation function.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{p_{l}}{p_{k}}=MRT_{lk}(y^{F}(p))
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(p=\lambda DF(y^{Y}(p))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 be a differentaible transformation function.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
 The Lagrangian to the profit maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=p\cdot y-\lambda(F(y))
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial y_{l}}=0:p_{l}-\lambda\frac{\partial F(y)}{\partial y_{l}}=0\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial y_{k}}=0:p_{k}-\lambda\frac{\partial F(y)}{\partial y_{k}}=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{p_{l}}{p_{k}}=\frac{\frac{\partial F(y)}{\partial y_{l}}}{\frac{\partial F(y)}{\partial y_{k}}}=MRT_{lk}(y^{F}(p))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Profit maximization: Production Function model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the production function single-output model we denote input prices by
 
\begin_inset Formula $w\equiv(w_{1},\ldots,w_{L-1})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 and output price by 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is described by means of a production funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, the firm's profit maximization problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}}}pf(z)-w\cdot z
\]

\end_inset

We simplify notation and denote 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{f}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{f}$
\end_inset

 the supply correspondence and profit functions associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $Y^{f}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(51).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable production function and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 its associated production set.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, and each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{w_{l}}{w_{k}}=MRTS_{lk}(y^{f}(p))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable production function and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 its associated production set.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
 The Lagrangian of the profit maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=pf(z)-w\cdot z+\lambda z
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial z_{l}}=p_{l}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{l}}(z)-w_{l}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if}\ z_{l}>0\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial z_{k}}=p_{k}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{k}}(z)-w_{k}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if}\ z_{k}>0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an interior solution, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{w_{l}}{w_{k}}=\frac{\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{l}}(z)}{\frac{\partial f}{\partial z_{k}}(z)}=MRTS_{lk}(y^{f}(p))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

