\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be the production function associated with a single-output technology,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be the associated
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z)\leq0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

We prove that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1},\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\alpha y\in Y\iff\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset

.
 For this 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 By definition of the production frontier, 
\begin_inset Formula $(-z,q)\in\partial f\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z)=0\right\} \subseteq Y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies CRS, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\alpha z,\alpha q)\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
